<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    The Windows Phone 8.1 page: Avalonia's ContentPage as the phone lays out a Page. docs/controls/Page/PLAN.md has the
    values and their sources.

    The phone's Page has no template (it is a UserControl): it fills its Frame, and so the window, tray shown or not,
    and its own background reaches under the tray. What the view takes off, the tray at the top and the app bar at the
    bottom (VisibleBounds), comes off its content: under UseVisible, the default, the content is laid out in what is
    left; under UseCoreWindow it fills the window (tools/refapp/README.md, "Visible bounds" and "The page's app bar").
    Here the safe area stands for the tray: AutomaticallyApplySafeAreaPadding (on by default) is UseVisible, off is
    UseCoreWindow. Avalonia's ContentPage puts the safe area on PART_ContentPresenter's Padding, so that presenter is the
    page's whole area here, drawing the background, and the command bars and the content sit inside it: the safe area
    takes the tray off the top, then the bars and the content share the rest.

    The bottom command bar is docked at the bottom of what is left and the content laid out above it, so the content
    gives up the bar's height, as the phone's VisibleBounds gives up the app bar's (62.92 for a compact bar, 26.25 for
    a minimal one, on the reference): how tall the bar is, the CommandBar's theme decides. The bars draw over the
    content (an open bar reaches up over it, as the phone's does, without the content moving). The phone has no top
    app bar to measure; the top command bar is docked at the top the same way.

    The phone has no navigation bar: the title is the page's own content, and Back is the hardware button. So a
    ContentPage in a NavigationPage has none (NavigationPage.HasNavigationBar off), and gets the whole safe area itself.
    An app that wants Avalonia's bar sets it on the page.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="{x:Type ContentPage}" TargetType="ContentPage">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource ApplicationPageBackgroundThemeBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource PhoneForegroundBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource PhoneFontFamilyNormal}" />
    <Setter Property="NavigationPage.HasNavigationBar" Value="False" />
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <ControlTemplate>
        <ContentPresenter x:Name="PART_ContentPresenter" Background="{TemplateBinding Background}">
          <DockPanel>
            <!-- Shown and filled from code, by Avalonia's ContentPage, when the page has the bar. -->
            <ContentPresenter x:Name="PART_TopCommandBar" DockPanel.Dock="Top" ZIndex="1"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch" IsVisible="False" />
            <ContentPresenter x:Name="PART_BottomCommandBar" DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" ZIndex="1"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch" IsVisible="False" />
            <!-- Inside PART_ContentPresenter's own content, so not the template's: bound to the page by ancestry. -->
            <ContentPresenter x:Name="PART_Content"
                              Content="{Binding Content, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ContentPage}}"
                              ContentTemplate="{Binding ContentTemplate, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ContentPage}}"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="{Binding HorizontalContentAlignment, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ContentPage}}"
                              VerticalContentAlignment="{Binding VerticalContentAlignment, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ContentPage}}" />
          </DockPanel>
        </ContentPresenter>
      </ControlTemplate>
    </Setter>
  </ControlTheme>
</ResourceDictionary>
